Flow narrowing of `option<T>` after a null test: in the branch where the value
cannot be null it is a `T`, which before this was not expressible at all --
`option` declares no methods and every reader in the library is a total
function taking a default.

The judgement is not purely type-level, which is the whole reason this is a
golden rather than a checker test. `option<T>` is a nullable representation on
two targets and a pointer on the third, so the read a null test earns is spelled
three ways: Go's `*(m.maybe)`, Kotlin's `maybe!!`, and JavaScript's bare read of
a value that is either the number or null. A backend that emitted the wrong one
would still type-check and still generate.

`&&` narrows what follows it in the same condition, which is why the second
branch reads the value twice -- once to test it and once as an int.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `int\(m\.maybe\)` -- Go's option<int> is *int, so the unwrap is the star and never a cast
deny * `maybe\.toInt\(\)` -- Kotlin's option<int> is Int?, and toInt() on one does not compile

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
    }
    none {
        html
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
}

component main node {
    var maybe option<int>

    button(text="clear", @click {
        maybe = null
    })
    if maybe != null {
        text(value=string(maybe * 2))
    }
    if maybe != null && maybe > 2 {
        text(value="big")
    }
}

window {
    main
}
